  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 40-year-old man with erectile dysfunction presents with two elevated fasting morning prolactin measurements (27 ng/mL, then 30 ng/mL, ref range 2-18 ng/mL). He has no visual symptoms, headache, or renal disease. He takes no medications or supplements. Does patient need a brain MRI for prolactinoma?",
    "recommendations": "Mild prolactin elevations rarely indicate prolactinoma. Although a pituitary MRI can assess for stalk effect or microadenoma, it is reasonable first to measure an afternoon prolactin and macroprolactin to exclude physiologic fluctuations and macroprolactin. If macroprolactin is elevated, imaging is unnecessary. Additionally, would be important to evaluate thyroid function tests (more common cause of prolactin elevation) and measure gonodotropin and testosterone levels as these can cause ED. If MRI reveals pituitary abnormalities, refer to endocrinology. Also consider other transient causes such as sleep, stress, or recent intercourse."
  },
